; KotinosOS admin-mode grant: its own SELinux type, and the one domain allowed
; to read it besides root.
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS
;
; The grant at /run/kotinos/admin-grant is what says admin mode is open. Both
; paths to root consult it: sudo through a PAM helper, and polkit through a rule
; that spawns the same checker. The PAM helper runs as root and can read it.
; polkitd cannot -- it runs as the unprivileged `polkitd` user in the
; policykit_t domain, and the grant sat in /run as var_run_t, which policykit_t
; has no read access to.
;
; That denial was invisible. A dontaudit rule suppressed it, so the polkit rule
; simply failed with nothing in the journal and nothing in `ausearch`; it only
; appeared after `semodule -DB`. Worth remembering the next time a policy
; problem presents as "the code does not work for no reason".
;
; WHY A DEDICATED TYPE RATHER THAN OPENING UP var_run_t
;
; Allowing policykit_t to read var_run_t would grant it every runtime state file
; on the system to fix one. A type of our own is the smallest change that makes
; the grant readable exactly where it must be: policykit_t may read it, nothing
; else gains anything, and the file is still unwritable by everything except
; root.
;
; The security property that matters here is INTEGRITY, not secrecy. Knowing
; that admin mode is open tells an attacker nothing they could not learn by
; trying sudo; being able to *write* this file would hand them root. So read
; access is granted narrowly and write access is granted to nobody new.

(type kotinos_admin_grant_t)
(typeattributeset file_type (kotinos_admin_grant_t))
(roletype object_r kotinos_admin_grant_t)

; polkit's rule engine spawns the grant checker, which reads this file.
(allow policykit_t kotinos_admin_grant_t (file (read open getattr)))

; It lives on the tmpfs mounted at /run, so the directory it sits in has to be
; traversable by the same domain.
(allow policykit_t var_run_t (dir (search getattr)))
